APR for Linux compiled to WebAssembly.

No fork(): there is no MMU, so fork() does not exist (unistd.h does not even declare it), and
configure finds none (APR_HAS_FORK 0). APR forks in three places:

- apr_proc_create (threadproc/unix/proc.c), the one Subversion uses (external diff and merge
  tools, hook scripts, svn+ssh's tunnel): the child now starts the way musl's posix_spawn starts
  one, with clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK) on a stack of its own, and does what the forked child
  did (stdio, working directory, group and user, resource limits, detaching, errfn on failure)
  in our memory. So apr_pool_cleanup_for_exec() does not run in it: it would take the cleanups
  off our pools, and APR's files, pipes and sockets are close-on-exec anyway.
- apr_proc_fork: APR_ENOTIMPL (svnserve, the one user here, takes threads without APR_HAS_FORK).
- apr_proc_detach(APR_PROC_DETACH_DAEMONIZE): no fork into the background, as on MPE and OS/2;
  the process still leaves its session and puts stdio on /dev/null.

And getopt's default errfn (misc/unix/getopt.c), fprintf cast to a function that returns void:
WebAssembly checks the type of a call through a pointer, and the cast one traps ("indirect call
type mismatch") on the first unknown option, e.g. in svnmucc. A wrapper of the right type.

